   {
    "id": "p1",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "content": [
     {
      "t": "text",
      "v": "In May 2024, hail as big as grapefruit fell in Texas. A month later, people found hailstones almost the size of pineapples. Similar records are being reported worldwide. Are hailstorms getting worse? Researchers say yes."
     }
    ]
   },
   {
    "id": "p2",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "content": [
     {
      "t": "text",
      "v": "A study from Northern Illinois University shows that larger, more damaging hail is becoming more common. This is one result of human-caused climate change. The research "
     },
     {
      "t": "vocab",
      "v": "predicts",
      "ref": "v-predict"
     },
     {
      "t": "text",
      "v": " that hailstones four centimetres or larger will appear 15% to 75% more often in the future, depending on how much "
     },
     {
      "t": "vocab",
      "v": "greenhouse gas",
      "ref": "v-greenhouse-gas"
     },
     {
      "t": "text",
      "v": " we "
     },
     {
      "t": "vocab",
      "v": "emit",
      "ref": "v-emit"
     },
     {
      "t": "text",
      "v": "."
     }
    ]
   },
   {
    "id": "p3",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "content": [
     {
      "t": "text",
      "v": "Hail forms when raindrops are lifted high into "
     },
     {
      "t": "vocab",
      "v": "thunderstorms",
      "ref": "v-thunderstorm"
     },
     {
      "t": "text",
      "v": ". Strong upward winds carry them into cold parts of the "
     },
     {
      "t": "vocab",
      "v": "atmosphere",
      "ref": "v-atmosphere"
     },
     {
      "t": "text",
      "v": ", where they "
     },
     {
      "t": "vocab",
      "v": "freeze",
      "ref": "v-freeze"
     },
     {
      "t": "text",
      "v": ". "
     },
     {
      "t": "vocab",
      "v": "Moisture",
      "ref": "v-moisture"
     },
     {
      "t": "text",
      "v": " in the air then sticks to the ice, making it grow layer by layer—like an onion."
     }
    ]
   },
   {
    "id": "p4",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "content": [
     {
      "t": "text",
      "v": "Why is climate change causing larger hail? Warmer air provides more energy, creating stronger upward winds. These powerful winds keep hailstones inside storms longer, allowing them to collect more ice. As study author Victor Gensini explained, “If you want to create a large hailstone, you really need a strong "
     },
     {
      "t": "gloss",
      "v": "updraft",
      "zh": "上升气流"
     },
     {
      "t": "text",
      "v": ".”"
     }
    ]
   },
   {
    "id": "p5",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "content": [
     {
      "t": "text",
      "v": "Interestingly, while large hail may become more common, small hail could become less "
     },
     {
      "t": "vocab",
      "v": "frequent",
      "ref": "v-frequent"
     },
     {
      "t": "text",
      "v": ". As temperatures rise, the freezing level moves higher, so smaller hailstones are more likely to melt before reaching the ground."
     }
    ]
   }
  ]
 },
